    "case_id": "715",
    "case_summary": "Male patient, born in 2013, with frequent respiratory episodes during first year of life. Initial sweat tests were uncertain (42-60 mmol/l). At 8-10 months, developed Pseudo-Bartter's syndrome (hypochloremia, hyponatremia, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis). Genetic testing at 12 months revealed F508del/3849 + 10kbC-T mutation. From December 2015 (age 2) to 2018, regular oropharyngeal samples showed bacterial presence in amounts of 102-104 colony-forming units.\n\nPhysical examination showed no significant aggravation from 2015 to 2022. Blood chemistry had no significant deviations, and vitamin D level was within normal range.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    - Regular sputum microbiological examinations from 2014 to 2022    - Regular PCR tests of sputum samples (at least 4 per year from 2016)    - Blood chemistry tests showing no significant deviations      - Imaging Studies:    - Computer tomography confirmed inflammatory alteration of the paranasal sinuses    - Lung computer tomography showed no signs of damage      - Microbiological Tests:    - February 2019: Sputum sample negative, nasal lavage fluid showed 32 colony-forming units/ml    - MALDI TOF mass spectrometry and whole genome sequencing confirmed taxonomic affiliation    - Protein profile analysis showed homogeneity of isolates from 2015-2020",
